---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from "../consts";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
    </head>

    <body>
        <Header />

        <main>
            <!-- Hero -->
            <section class="hero">
                <h1>I TURN COMPLEXITY INTO STRUCTURE</h1>

                <p class="hero-description">
                    I build backend systems for problems that aren't always
                    straightforward.
                </p>

                <p class="hero-description">
                    I like understanding how things fit together, figuring out
                    what actually matters, and turning that understanding into
                    software that works reliably in production.
                </p>

                <a href="#approach">&darr; KEEP READING</a>
            </section>

            <!-- Engineering approach -->
            <section class="problem" id="approach">
                <h2>WHEN EVERYTHING IS CONNECTED, NOTHING IS OBVIOUS</h2>

                <div>
                    <p>
                        Requirements. Constraints. Legacy decisions. Distributed
                        systems. Real-time data. Hardware. Protocols.
                        Performance.
                    </p>

                    <p>Everything affects everything else.</p>

                    <p>
                        Understanding those relationships is often harder than
                        writing the code itself.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        <strong>
                            The hardest engineering problems aren't solved by
                            writing more code.
                        </strong>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        They're solved by understanding the system well enough
                        to know <strong>
                            what code should exist in the first place.</strong
                        >
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Engineering Notebook -->
            <section class="notebook">
                <h2>HOW I THINK ABOUT ENGINEERING</h2>

                <div>
                    <p>
                        The Engineering Notebook is where I explore the
                        decisions behind real systems.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        The constraints, trade-offs, unexpected problems and
                        reasoning that don't always fit into a project summary.
                        Not just what I built, but why I built it that way.
                    </p>

                    <a href="/notebook"
                        >Explore the Engineering Notebook &rarr;</a
                    >
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- About -->
            <section class="about">
                <h2>A LITTLE ABOUT ME</h2>

                <p>I like understanding how things work.</p>

                <p>
                    Not just the code, but the whole thing: what the system is
                    trying to achieve, what constraints it has, where the pieces
                    connect, and what happens when something inevitably goes
                    wrong.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I've spent my career building software in different
                    environments, from backend platforms and data-processing
                    systems to software running on industrial hardware.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Some of those systems were well understood from the
                    beginning. Others weren't.
                </p>

                <p>I've always found the second kind more interesting.</p>

                <p><strong>The messy ones. The difficult ones.</strong></p>

                <p>
                    The ones where you need to step back, make sense of what's
                    there, and figure out where to go next.
                </p>

                <a href="/about">More about me &rarr;</a>
            </section>

            <!-- Contact -->
            <section class="contact" id="contact">
                <h2>LET'S TALK ENGINEERING</h2>

                <p>
                    I'm interested in complex backend systems, data processing,
                    performance, and the engineering decisions that make
                    software reliable in production.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If you're working on something challenging, or simply want
                    to exchange ideas about engineering, feel free to get in
                    touch.
                </p>

                <a href="mailto:josemaria@jmssalas.com">
                    Get in touch &rarr;
                </a>
            </section>
        </main>

        <Footer />
    </body>
</html>
